# Line endings.
#
# Without this file git uses core.autocrlf, which on this machine is `input`:
# it never converts on checkout, so a file saved by a Windows editor keeps its
# CRLF in the working tree while the blob stays LF. `git status` then reports
# clean, because the normalization round-trip looks identical -- and the packed
# zip (built from the LF blob) quietly disagrees with the extracted directory
# (built from the CRLF worktree). Every `git diff --no-index` against
# release/ used to warn about exactly this.
#
# Declaring the endings here makes the choice explicit and identical on every
# checkout, so a repackaged zip and its extracted directory compare byte-clean.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.abap text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf

# Not text. The zips are the shipped artifacts; treating one as text would
# re-encode it and invalidate its SHA256.
*.zip -text
*.pyc -text
